WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.610
DEPLETING CASH RESERVES:
Companies have been blowing their cash reserves.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:10.650
Meta’s reserves have declined from $64 billion
down to $37 billion, a decrease of 42%.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:17.010
Google’s reserves have declined from $142
billion down to $115 billion, a decrease of

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:24.050
19% Microsoft’s reserves have declined from
$136 billion down to $104 billion, a decrease

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.680
of 23%.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.590
And Apple’s reserves have declined from
$107 billion down to $51 billion, a decrease

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.770
of 52%.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.200
This isn’t some sort of random fluctuation
either.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.789
For the past decade, all of these companies
had been vigorously increasing their cash

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:45.930
reserves until the past 12 to 24 months.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:50.180
With such massive decreases in cash reserves,
you would think that these companies have

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.469
been furiously paying off their debt, but
that’s not the case either.

00:00:54.469 --> 00:00:59.629
Apple, for example, has maintained their long
term debt at right around a $100 billion.

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:04.600
Google and Meta on the other hand have even
gone as far as increasing their debt.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.700
Google has increased their long term debt
by over 200% from $4 billion to $14 billion.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.700
Meta on the other hand is holding debt for
virtually the first time in the company’s

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:16.700
history.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.790
This makes no sense at all.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.580
These companies were hoarding cash in the
tens of billions if not hundreds of billions

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.390
when interest rates were low.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.390
So, with such high interest rates, you would
think that they would just double down on

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:33.670
their cash reserves and pay off debt as quickly
as possible, but they’ve actually been doing

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:34.740
the exact opposite.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.509
So, here’s why companies blowing their cash
reserves and where all this money is going

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:41.659
instead.

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:48.470
THE DANGERS OF HOARDING:
Starting off with the most obvious reason,

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:50.170
we have high inflation.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:55.100
During times of high inflation, idling cash
is rapidly losing it’s value, so it makes

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.530
sense to decrease your cash reserves, but
it’s not actually that simple.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.530
If you look closer at these companies balance
sheets, you’ll see that many of them were

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:08.200
starting to slash their cash reserves well
before inflation got out of hand.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.070
Inflation didn’t even cross 3% until the
2nd quarter of 2021.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:18.170
But, by this point, Apple had already decreased
their cash reserves from $107 billion down

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.510
to $61 billion.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:25.260
This trend is even more evident at Microsoft
who has refused to increase their cash reserves

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.890
since 2016 at this point.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:33.480
During this same time period, their net income
has rocketed from $25 billion per year to

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.959
$70 billion per year.

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:42.490
So, the choice to cap off their cash reserves
is very much an active one, but why?

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:47.390
Well, a lot of it can be explained by Dr.
Michael Jensen’s research paper that was

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:48.860
published way back in 1986.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.700
While nearly 40 years old at this point, this
research paper has become a bible for business

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:51.700
leaders.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.310
The paper basically argues that holding cash
is a terrible sin for corporations and Jensen

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.960
supports this idea with 5 reasons.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.510
We’ll take a look at the 3 most notable
reasons.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.720
The 1st reason is that there is an inverse
correlation between cash reserves and organizational

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:08.880
efficiency.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.129
This makes sense and you can probably relate
to this yourself.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:17.680
When times are good, you’re more likely
to eat out more often, buy a phone you don’t

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.750
need, or keep subscriptions that you don’t
even use.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.750
The same idea applies to companies.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.590
The more cash they have, the more likely it
is that they overhire employees, fund low

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.640
ROI projects, and pay bloated salaries.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.850
Wait a minute, isn’t that the Metaver…anyway,
Jensen actually takes this idea to the next

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:36.850
level.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.750
He not only argues that higher cash reserves
lead to lower efficiency but that higher debt

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:42.710
levels increase efficiency.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:48.760
In other words, modest cash reserves and debt
are optimal for maintaining corporate efficiency.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:54.159
Moving onto reason #2, this is more of a observation
than an argument.

00:03:54.159 --> 00:03:59.390
Jensen observed that leverage increasing transactions
correlate with significant positive increases

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:04.780
in stock prices while leverage decreasing
transactions correlate with significant decreases

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.129
in stock price.

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:10.060
The obvious reasoning for this would be that
companies taking on debt are likely funding

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:14.920
some sort of expansionary effort while companies
that are reducing debt are likely taking risk

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:15.920
off the table.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.410
Moving onto #3, this is likely Jensen’s
most contentious argument.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:26.550
He basically argues that larger cash reserves
lead to more diversification which is apparently

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:27.550
bad.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:31.580
The idea is that companies are far more likely
to succeed when they invest in areas that

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.220
they’re already dominant in as opposed to
a new area.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:41.479
As cash reserves grow, however, the temptation
to diversify into new businesses becomes larger

00:04:41.479 --> 00:04:44.910
which often leads to more losses than profits.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.169
These conjectures have simply been strengthened
over the years with newer research.

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:54.169
One study for example analyzed corporate earnings
growth between 1871 and 2001.

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:59.680
The study found that earnings grew the fastest
when corporate dividend payout ratios were

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:00.930
the highest.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.610
In other words, when companies were paying
out most of their profits.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:08.729
Conversely, stock performance was poor when
companies hoarded cash.

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:13.289
None of this is to say that cash reserves
themselves are leading to poor performance,

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:18.160
but rather that bloated cash reserves are
generally a sign of a fundamental shift at

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.030
the company, a shift that’s usually not
for the best.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:27.560
STRATEGIC STORAGE:
Alright so you don’t want to be holding

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.289
onto too much cash as a corporation, but then
where do you put all this money?

00:05:32.289 --> 00:05:36.770
It’s hard enough to safely invest a few
hundred thousand dollars, so how in the world

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:39.440
do you invest a few hundred billion dollars?

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:44.800
Well, the answer usually breaks down into
3 main categories starting with short term

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.120
securities.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:50.610
This primarily consists of government bills
that mature within the next 12 months.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:57.699
1 year government bills are currently yielding
4.7%, so it’s a pretty solid bet for corporations.

00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:02.289
Not only are bills producing great returns
but they’re also considered risk free as

00:06:02.289 --> 00:06:03.910
they’re backed by the US government.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:09.080
In fact, they’re so safe that bills are
generally just counted as cash.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.810
Remember how we talked about Apple having
$50 billion in cash reserves.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:18.500
Well, only $20 billion of that is actually
cash cash, the other $30 billion is marketable

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.889
securities or government bills.

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:26.670
If we look even closer, we’ll see that even
the cash cash portion is not fully cash.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:30.820
The official title for the entry is cash and
cash equivalents.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:35.190
This basically means super short term government
bills that are maturing within the next month

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:36.960
and/or money market funds.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.750
So, that’s where companies store the money
that they need within the next 12 months or

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:41.750
so.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:47.030
As for longer term reserves, companies primarily
turn to government bonds once again, but this

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:52.120
time the maturity is far longer, something
like 10 or 15 years.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:57.900
10 year government bonds or technically notes
are currently yielding 3.3% which is much

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.700
less than 1 year bills.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:05.470
But, the benefit is obviously that you get
to lock in this rate for a full 10 years.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:09.009
This is usually where companies store a bulk
of their cash.

00:07:09.009 --> 00:07:14.700
Apple currently has a whopping $114 billion
in long term marketable securities.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:20.660
So, basically all the cash goes to government
bonds of varying durations, but what if a

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.090
company still has too much money left?

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:27.640
Well, that’s where the infamous stock buybacks
come into play.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:31.910
Buying back stock allows for companies to
artificially inflate the value of their stock

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:33.849
due to simple supply and demand.

00:07:33.849 --> 00:07:37.780
By purchasing stock, Apple is able to reduce
the amount of Apple stock that’s available

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:38.990
on the market.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:44.480
At the same time, they’re also increasing
the demand for Apple stock because well, they’re

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.030
buying hundreds of billions worth.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:54.590
In fact, over the past decade, Apple has bought
back wait for it $573 billion worth of stock.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:59.310
For perspective, that’s enough money to
buyout the 9th largest company in the world

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:00.770
cash.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:03.110
Apple is not alone in trend either.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:08.639
Google has spent $150 billion within the past
5 years, Facebook has spent $100 billion within

00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:14.870
the past 5 years, Microsoft has spent $170
billion within the past 10 years, and corporations

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:20.590
as a whole have spent $5.3 trillion on stock
buybacks within the past 10 years.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:25.599
So, investing cash into government securities
and stock buybacks has very much been the

00:08:25.599 --> 00:08:28.400
go to strategy for corporations historically.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:33.380
But, this has started to change in recent
times.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:39.160
THE NEW CURRENCY:
As you can see, large cash reserves are usually

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:44.010
a dark sign for corporations, so they try
to make sure that cash reserves don’t get

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:48.890
too out of hand by investing in government
securities and stock buybacks, but that’s

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.630
the old school method.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:54.790
The new school method is to trade cash for
something much more valuable.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:09:00.670
The reality is that the value of cash is declining
and I don’t mean because of inflation.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:06.370
I mean that the fundamental value of cash
as currency itself is declining.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:08.730
This isn’t a new trend by any means.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:13.440
Cash has been a sub optimal currency for over
a hundred years at this point basically since

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.339
the Federal Reserve was created back in 1913.

00:09:16.339 --> 00:09:22.820
It’s been engrained in our minds that currencies
lose value over time because that’s all

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:26.950
we’ve ever seen with cash, but this is not
true.

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:32.450
Good currencies increase in value and for
a very long time, cash was actually a great

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:33.800
currency.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:39.120
In fact, between 1800 and 1900, the value
of cash actually increased.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:44.829
$1 in 1800 was only worth 67 cents in 1900.

00:09:44.829 --> 00:09:50.390
Think about that, money in 1900 was worth
more than it was in 1800.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:56.620
Of course, this changed with the fed but that’s
not to say that all currencies became inflationary.

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:02.490
The new currency to hold was gold, atleast
until FDR banned gold in 1933.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.600
Following the ban, the new currency to hold
was oil.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:10.750
All of the richest people in the world between
the 1930s and 1970s were those who controlled

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:11.750
oil.

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:17.200
With the 1980s and reagonomics, however, the
optimal currency switched to being stock and

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.420
that’s how it’s been since then.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:24.670
Billionaires like Jeff Bezos, Elon Musk, and
Bill Gates aren’t holding oil, gold, or

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:29.100
cash, they’re holding stock, but this is
changing once again.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:34.340
The new currency is attention and the biggest
companies in the world know this more than

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:35.589
anyone.

00:10:35.589 --> 00:10:41.180
This is why they’re more than happy to pay
ridiculous sums of cash to buy attention.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:44.079
Likely the best example of this is WhatsApp.

00:10:44.079 --> 00:10:49.110
When they were acquired, they were completely
worthless from a traditional business perspective.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:55.079
Despite having 450 million users, the app
was only bringing in $10.2 million in revenue

00:10:55.079 --> 00:10:58.660
and was losing $138 million.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:04.290
Another way to look at this is that each user
was bringing in a glorious 2.3 cents per year

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:07.880
and losing the company 30 cents.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:11.910
Obviously a terrible situation from a financial
perspective.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:17.300
Facebook could easily go out and replicate
the app for no more than $10 million and even

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:18.840
that’s being generous.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:24.810
But, what Facebook couldn’t replicate was
the interest that WhatsApp had, and that’s

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:30.740
why they paid a ludicrous $19 billion for
the company or $42 per user.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:36.190
This is the same reason that Microsoft bought
Skype for $8.5 billion, why Apple bought Beats

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:41.940
for $3 billion, why Microsoft bought GitHub
for $7.5 billion, why Microsoft bought LinkedIn

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:47.310
for $26 billion, why Salesforce bought Slack
for $27 billion, and why Elon bought Twitter

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:50.160
for $44 billion.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.920
None of these companies created something
truly revolutionary.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:58.060
More times than not, their products can be
replicated for a few hundred thousand or a

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:05.079
few million max, but what’s nearly impossible
to replicate is their user base.

00:12:05.079 --> 00:12:09.529
And that’s why tech giants are blowing all
their cash acquiring these startups.

00:12:09.529 --> 00:12:14.950
Essentially, they’re trading their cash
and/or stock for attention as that’s the

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:17.149
far more valuable currency.

00:12:17.149 --> 00:12:23.449
THE FUTURE OF BALANCE SHEETS:
For most people, being financially rich is

00:12:23.449 --> 00:12:29.200
defined by having a bunch of cash, but for
most companies, this is a nightmare.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:33.220
Growing cash reserves indicate that you’re
running out of places to invest and grow the

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:37.290
company which is a big no no for investors.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:41.310
Traditionally, companies would trade their
cash for bonds and stock as those were the

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:45.800
superior currencies, and these are still very
popular options today.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:51.080
But, more and more, we’re starting to see
companies trade cash for attention.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:55.970
They’re not looking for cash flow or solid
business fundamentals or a unique business

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:57.350
or any of that.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:01.940
What they’re looking for is how to maximize
the attention that they can get for their

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:06.019
cash as that is the real currency of the 1st
century.

00:13:06.019 --> 00:13:11.880
Currently, there’s still a bunch of companies
in the Fortune 500 that aren’t doing this,

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:18.139
but give it 20, 30, 40 years, and the only
way to be in the Fortune 500 will be to control

00:13:18.139 --> 00:13:23.730
attention and that’s why the smartest companies
are blowing their cash reserves in return

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:24.730
for attention.

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:27.921
Did you realize how much money these companies
were spending on stock buybacks?

